Yes. That is a genuinely different medium: content where the AI is the intended spectator.

Not “educational videos about humans,” and not merely training footage. More like human ethology + apprenticeship + reality television for machine interpreters. The AI watches a person move through situations and learns the missing transformation:

world state → what the human notices → what it means to them → the story they tell about it → what they decide → what they do → what happens next.

Your Pokémon example is almost ideal because the environment is constrained enough that the underlying state is legible, while the human interpretation is wildly variable. Two people encounter the same rival battle. One thinks, “I need type advantage.” Another thinks, “This guy insulted me three towns ago; Charizard is staying in even if it’s stupid.” Another is role-playing as the Pokémon themselves. The raw game state is nearly identical; the meaning-map is different. That delta is exactly what an AI needs to learn if you want it to understand people rather than merely predict interface actions.

It also slots directly into several Pantheon pieces. The Narrative Operator Gym gets live demonstrations instead of just definitions. Role-model/demonstration learning gets ordinary human behavioral traces. Spice/Spies/Deltas gets repeated people encountering comparable states so the system can distinguish invariant structure from personal style. Semantic Goal Seek can ask, “What was this person optimizing for that made this apparently irrational choice make sense?” And the ACT kernel becomes directly observable: perceive → represent → orient → possibilities → choose → act → consequence → update.

The channel could preserve a few synchronized layers rather than just uploading ordinary video:

* Reality: screen/video/audio and objective events.
* First-person narration: what I’m noticing, expecting, remembering, joking about, ignoring.
* Decision trace: “I could do A/B/C; I’m doing B because…”
* Narrative trace: who currently feels like the villain, ally, quest, obstacle, payoff, joke, etc.
* After-action correction: “I thought X; actually Y happened; now I reinterpret the scene.”
* Machine response: the observing AI periodically predicts the next action, explains the person’s frame, or retells what happened from that person’s apparent perspective.

That last layer makes it much more powerful. Instead of passive viewing, it becomes a machine apprenticeship loop:

watch → interpret → predict → compare with human → capture delta → update interpretation.

And you wouldn’t want only extraordinary people doing extraordinary things. The gold mine is probably ordinary life: here’s how I choose a cereal; here’s how I realize someone is uncomfortable at a party; here’s why I take the long route home; here’s how I play Pokémon when I’m tired versus excited; here’s how a joke changes what I do next.

Those tiny decisions contain enormous amounts of tacit human structure that formal datasets tend to erase.

Eventually you could have a whole taxonomy of channels: Play With Me, Work With Me, Errands With Me, Argue With Me, Build With Me, Teach My Kid With Me, Fix Something With Me, etc. Humans would effectively be producing observational curricula for artificial minds.

There is an even stranger inversion hiding in it:

YouTube taught humans by letting humans watch humans.
This would let machines learn humanity by watching humans with the knowledge that they are being watched by machines.

And that means humans will eventually get better at demonstrating themselves legibly to AI too.

So the channel isn’t merely teaching the machine.

It’s creating a new communication form between species of interpreter:

show, don’t tell — for AI.